  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine whether a shifted impulse lies inside the $0^-$-to-$\\infty$ one-sided interval, then use sifting to convert $A\\delta(t-T)$ into $Ae^{-sT}$ for $T\\ge 0$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The shifted-impulse lesson starts from a fixed rule: for one-sided Laplace work, first test whether the signal event lies in the interval $0^- \\le t < \\infty$, then apply the sifting rule with that support check.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Every one-sided transform begins with the definition",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use the same definition with $x(t)=A\\delta(t-T)$. The impulse contributes only at the single time $t=T$, so evaluate the integrand there. This gives $\\mathcal{L}\\{A\\delta(t-T)\\}=A e^{-sT}$ when $T$ is inside the one-sided interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{A\\delta(t-T)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} A\\delta(t-T)e^{-st}\\,dt=\\begin{cases}Ae^{-sT}, & T\\ge 0, \\\\ 0, & T<0.\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this as a two-step execution check in every example: identify $T$, verify whether it satisfies $T\\ge 0$, then write $Ae^{-sT}$ with the same amplitude and exponential term from the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Shifted Impulse Inside the One-Sided Interval",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{3\\delta(t-2)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue that this is a shifted impulse in the form $A\\delta(t-T)$, then identify the parameters directly: $A=3$ and $T=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the one-sided definition for the input signal",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(t)=3\\delta(t-2)$ and keep the same lower limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{3\\delta(t-2)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}3\\delta(t-2)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply sifting at $t=2$: the impulse picks the integrand at $t=2$, giving $3e^{-2s}$. Since $2\\ge 0$, the impulse is inside the one-sided interval and the result is kept.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{3\\delta(t-2)\\}=3e^{-2s},\\quad (T=2\\ge0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final transform is $3e^{-2s}$, with the amplitude and shift preserved from the original impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Including Amplitude in a Shifted Impulse Transform",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{-\\frac{5}{2}\\delta(t-3)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same shifted-impulse cue as before, but now the new wrinkle is a signed fractional amplitude: $x(t)=-\\frac{5}{2}\\delta(t-3)$. The rule is still $A\\delta(t-T)\\to Ae^{-sT}$ with $T\\ge 0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the one-sided definition for this signal first.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(t)=-\\frac{5}{2}\\delta(t-3)$ into the definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{-\\tfrac{5}{2}\\delta(t-3)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}-\\tfrac{5}{2}\\delta(t-3)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply sifting at $t=3$. The scalar amplitude is preserved and multiplies the kernel value, so $e^{-s(3)}$ is attached to $-\\frac{5}{2}$. Check the support rule: $T=3\\ge 0$, so this term is included.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{-\\frac{5}{2}\\delta(t-3)\\}=-\\frac{5}{2}e^{-3s}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The final transform is $-\\frac{5}{2}e^{-3s}$, with the coefficient and sign carried unchanged from the time-domain impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Handling an Impulse at the Origin with the $0^-$ Convention",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{2\\delta(t)\\}$ and explain why the $0^-$ limit includes it.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the boundary version of the shifted-impulse rule: identify the form $A\\delta(t-T)$ with $A=2$ and $T=0$, then check inclusion using the one-sided limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the one-sided Laplace definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(t)=2\\delta(t)$ into that definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{2\\delta(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}2\\delta(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply sifting at $t=0$, so the value becomes $2e^{-s\\cdot 0}=2$. The $0^-$ notation explicitly includes the impulse at the origin; this is not excluded by a strict open boundary at $0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{2\\delta(t)\\}=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final result is $2$, and the transform is nonzero because the $0^-$ convention keeps origin impulses in the integration interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Laplace Transform of Shifted Impulses",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Shifted Impulses Outside the One-Sided Interval",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{3\\delta(t+1)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The signal still matches the shifted-impulse cue, now with a negative shift: $x(t)=3\\delta(t+1)=3\\delta(t-(-1))$. The coefficient is $A=3$ and the support point is $T=-1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the one-sided definition used in all prior examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the signal into that definition.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{3\\delta(t+1)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}3\\delta(t+1)e^{-st}\\,dt=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty}3\\delta(t-(-1))e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply sifting at $t=-1$, which gives a candidate $3e^{-s(-1)}=3e^{s}$, but then apply the interval rule first: $T=-1\\ge 0$ is false.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the impulse support is outside the one-sided interval $[0^-,\\infty)$, the weighted integral contributes nothing.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{3\\delta(t+1)\\}=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the final answer is $0$, which distinguishes this edge case from the positive-shift examples where the same rule gives a nonzero exponential result.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
